{
  "format": "wspradar.config",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "profile": {
    "id": "vanhamel_rx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "Dr. Vanhamel et al. RX Antenna Rotation: Reproducing Figure 6’s 3 dB ΔSNR Step"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Publication: Vanhamel, J., Machiels, W., & Lamy, H. (2022). Using the WSPR Mode for Antenna Performance Evaluation and Propagation Assessment on the 160-m Band](https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/4809313)\n\nApplies a +1.6 dB Reference SNR Correction, rounding the 1.573591164 dB calibration documented on the [authors’ website](https://sites.google.com/myuba.be/wspr-station-compare/home/wspr-antenna-comparison-instrument), and compares the same transmitter decoded by ON4AWM0 and ON4AWM1 in the same WSPR cycle. The [authors’ receiver-correction instructions](https://sites.google.com/myuba.be/wspr-station-compare/home/wspr-station-compare-app/wspr-station-compare-add-on?pli=1&authuser=0#h.8110v9yak2eo) provide further reference for applying this offset. The paper text reports 1.2 dB; the 1.6 dB reconstruction agrees closely with Figure 6, without establishing which correction generated the published figure. In Segment Insight, check RX Benchmark Temporal → Δ SNR over Time: the pooled Full Range | All Directions view shows the broader upward transition, although it is not station-balanced. For the direct Figure 6 comparison, use Selected Station Evidence: M7AEO (IO82) → Δ SNR over Time (12h bins). Across 1,441 joint spots, median ΔSNR (ON4AWM0 ITA LWA II − corrected ON4AWM1 adapted shorted dipole) changes from −0.6 dB before 8 May to +2.4 dB afterward, a reconstructed 3 dB median step consistent with the paper’s upward transition following the 90° antenna rotation. Figure 6 uses reception number rather than UTC time; overlaying the chronologically ordered paired differences aligns seven independently read peaks and troughs within one reception and 0.1 dB, inside the graphical readout tolerances.\n\nThis profile’s active result is RX Benchmark only. A separate no-benchmark Performance run with the same Target and time window can provide supporting propagation context. In a historical separate Performance analysis, M7AEO’s Station Decode Rate + Evidence over Time (3h) and Target SNR showed night-classified opportunities at a 98% conditional Decode Rate and −10 dB median successful Target SNR at 30 dBm, versus 44% and −20 dB in daylight — consistent with Figure 6’s pronounced day/night variation."
    }
  },
